---
import Artwork from "@/components/Artwork.astro"
import CampaignPartners from "@/components/CampaignPartners.astro"
import CampaignStatus from "@/components/CampaignStatus.astro"
import LegalRoute from "@/components/LegalRoute.astro"
import MetaPage from "@/components/MetaPage.astro"
import StoryScene from "@/components/StoryScene.astro"
import TimelineRail from "@/components/TimelineRail.astro"
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro"
import { campaignStatus } from "@/data/campaign"
import { homeSteps } from "@/data/history"
---

<Layout>
  <MetaPage slot="head" />
  <article class="campaign landing">
    <header class="hero">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <CampaignStatus language="en" />
        <h1>
          DAC8 records every crypto customer.
          <span>We are fighting back.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Since 2026, exchanges and brokers in Austria have had to prepare a
          yearly record for the tax authority of how much you buy, sell,
          exchange and move to your own wallets. They do this for all customers,
          without any suspicion.
        </p>
        <nav class="hero-actions" aria-label="Get started">
          <a class="button primary" href="/en/get-involved/#spenden"
            >Support the case</a
          >
          <a class="button" href="/en/dossier/">What DAC8 requires</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
      <Artwork
        id="collectionReview"
        language="en"
        size="hero"
        priority
        caption="Our planned complaint targets the blanket collection of all customers’ data."
      />
    </header>
    <section class="brief" aria-label="DAC8 in Austria">
      <p class="brief-text">
        This is financial mass surveillance, and your provider stores it all for
        years, along with your ID and wallet addresses.
        <strong
          >We are convinced this violates your fundamental right to financial
          privacy, and we are preparing a legal challenge.</strong
        >
      </p>
      <dl class="keyfigures">
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>Customers</strong>
            are covered, even if their data is not ultimately reported.
          </dt>
          <dd>All</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>Retention by the provider</strong>
            for each annual report. A new one is added every year.
          </dt>
          <dd>10 <small>years</small></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>First report</strong>
            to the tax authority, covering the 2026 reporting year.
          </dt>
          <dd><time datetime="2027-07-31">31 July 2027</time></dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>
    <section id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <h2 id="problem-heading">What DAC8 does with your data</h2>
      <div class="story">
        <StoryScene
          art="accountToRecord"
          language="en"
          title="Your account becomes a file."
        >
          <p>
            Your name, address, tax identification number and date of birth sit
            next to your annual totals, for each crypto-asset separately: what
            you bought, sold, exchanged and moved to your own wallets.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="massCapture"
          language="en"
          title="Everyone recorded. Without suspicion."
        >
          <p>
            Providers prepare this data for all customers, including those who
            will not ultimately be reported. Austrian law requires this, going
            beyond the EU directive.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="yearsOnFile"
          language="en"
          title="More about you. Every year."
        >
          <p>
            Every year adds another report, and the provider keeps each one for
            ten years. It also holds your ID, your wallet addresses and every
            single transaction, for up to ten years after the customer
            relationship ends.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/dossier/#gemeldet"
        >What exactly is reported and what the provider keeps</a
      >
    </section>
    <section id="risiko" aria-labelledby="risiko-heading">
      <h2 id="risiko-heading">What it can lead to</h2>
      <div class="story">
        <StoryScene art="dataHack" language="en" title="Stored. Hacked. Sold.">
          <p>
            The more data sits in one place, and the longer it stays there, the
            more a break-in pays. Exchanges, identity verification services and
            tax administrations have already lost such data, to hackers and to
            bribed staff.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="profileFourSources"
          language="en"
          title="Every leak sharpens your profile."
          wide
        >
          <p>
            A public profile shows your face, a leak your wallet address, a
            hacked tax service your holdings. Put together, the pieces reveal
            what no single source holds: who you are, where you live and what
            you probably own.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="rankedPortrait"
          language="en"
          title="The more you move, the higher you rank."
        >
          <p>
            In Austria, fraudsters send fake tax-office messages in bulk to find
            out who holds crypto. A breach at a provider would hand them that
            list ready-made, sortable by how much each person moves. Whether the
            estimate is right does not matter to criminals.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="targetAtHome"
          language="en"
          title="Your profile. Your address. A target."
        >
          <p>
            This is organised crime with a division of labour. In the United
            States, a gang hacked databases, picked out people believed to hold
            crypto and broke into their homes when fraudulent calls did not
            work. In the first half of 2026, the French interior ministry
            counted 77 kidnappings, unlawful detentions and extortion cases
            involving crypto-assets.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/dossier/#problem">Why it is a problem</a>
    </section>
    <section id="faelle" aria-labelledby="faelle-heading">
      <h2 id="faelle-heading">This has already happened</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        Providers and authorities lose data on people’s wealth again and again.
        Fraudsters and violent criminals use it.
      </p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>
          <strong>Ledger, 2020: the addresses of 272,000 wallet buyers.</strong>
          <p>
            Names, postal addresses and phone numbers leak. Phishing, extortion
            threats and letters to home addresses asking for the recovery phrase
            follow.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Coinbase, 2025: defrauded with real customer data.</strong>
          <p>
            Bribed support staff copy customer data. Fraudsters use it to pose
            as Coinbase and persuade customers to send them money. Coinbase
            expects costs, including reimbursements, of up to 400 million
            dollars.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Waltio, 2026: tax data as a target list.</strong>
          <p>
            Customers’ gains, losses and year-end balances are stolen from the
            French crypto tax service. According to the Gendarmerie, criminals
            use them to select victims of violent crime.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            >France, 2026: the tax administration is breached twice.</strong
          >
          <p>
            Using officials’ stolen credentials, attackers take 1.2 million
            records from the bank-account register and tax data on 678,000
            individuals and businesses.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="prose attacks-teaser">
        All over the world, criminals rob, kidnap and torture people they take
        for crypto holders, and there are more such cases every year.
        <a href="/en/attacks/">Open the attack archive</a>
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/dossier/#faelle">More cases and sources</a>
    </section>
    <section id="geschichte" aria-labelledby="geschichte-heading">
      <h2 id="geschichte-heading">How we got here</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        DAC8 is the eighth step of an EU directive whose predecessor dates from
        1977. Assistance in individual cases has turned into annual reports on
        salaries, bank accounts, platform sales and crypto-assets.
      </p>
      <TimelineRail language="en" steps={homeSteps} />
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/history/">The full history since 1977</a>
    </section>
    <section id="weg" aria-labelledby="weg-heading">
      <h2 id="weg-heading">What we are doing</h2>
      <div class="columns">
        <p class="section-intro">
          <strong
            >We are convinced that the blanket collection of all customers’ data
            violates fundamental rights.</strong
          >
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          {campaignStatus.en.text}
        </p>
      </div>
      <LegalRoute language="en" />
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/proceedings/">Where the case stands</a>
    </section>
    <section id="handeln" aria-labelledby="handeln-heading">
      <h2 id="handeln-heading">How you can help</h2>
      <div class="act">
        <div class="act-lead">
          <Artwork id="financeTheCase" language="en" caption={false} />
          <div>
            <h3>Help fund the case</h3>
            <p>Your donation pays for the proceedings.</p>
            <a class="button primary" href="/en/get-involved/#spenden"
              >Donate</a
            >
          </div>
        </div>
        <ul class="act-list">
          <li>
            <a href="/en/get-involved/#abgeordnete">
              <strong>Write to your MPs</strong>
              <span
                >A letter to the National Council, drafted in your browser and
                sent by you.</span
              >
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/en/proceedings/">
              <strong>Follow the case</strong>
              <span>We publish news and next steps here and via RSS.</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/en/get-involved/#teilen">
              <strong>Share the campaign</strong>
              <span
                >Ready-made graphics on the story, in German and English.</span
              >
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="vision" class="vision" aria-labelledby="vision-heading">
      <h2 id="vision-heading" class="statement">
        Privacy needs no justification.
        <span>State interference does.</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        A liberal democracy needs limits on state power. Where there is specific
        suspicion of a crime, authorities should investigate that case, with a
        legal basis and independent oversight. We reject collecting everyone’s
        financial data as a precaution.
      </p>
      <div class="vision-points">
        <div>
          <h3>What we aim to achieve</h3>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>Overturn disproportionate obligations</li>
            <li>Limit data collection</li>
            <li>Secure effective protection for those affected</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="precedent">
          <h3>Why we are confident</h3>
          <p class="precedent-year" aria-hidden="true">2014</p>
          <div>
            <p>
              In 2014, the Court of Justice struck down the data retention
              directive and Austria’s Constitutional Court struck down the
              national law. That is why we combine concrete proceedings with
              political advocacy: in court and with the representatives who can
              change these laws.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="position" aria-labelledby="position-heading">
      <h2 id="position-heading">Who we are</h2>
      <div class="about">
        <div>
          <p class="section-intro">
            dac8.org is an initiative by Bitcoin Austria, the nonprofit Bitcoin
            association founded in Vienna in 2011. We are Bitcoiners and
            cypherpunks. We see privacy as a prerequisite for a free society.
          </p>
          <p class="prose">
            The campaign concerns everyone covered by DAC8, regardless of which
            crypto-assets they use. It starts in Austria, but DAC8 applies
            across the EU: if you want to challenge the law in your country, get
            in touch. We share arguments, sources and experience:
            <a href="mailto:kontakt@dac8.org">kontakt@dac8.org</a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <CampaignPartners />
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
